---
scope: src/tools/init/injectBadge
description: >
  Init category that injects a "Built with AIDE" shield badge into the host
  project's README.md, skipping silently when no README exists or the badge
  is already present.
intent: >
  injectBadge gives AIDE-initialized projects a visible signal of adoption.
  When a host project has a README.md, this init category inserts a shields.io
  "AIDE — intent-driven" badge at the top of the file, placed after any
  existing badge row so it blends into an established badge strip rather than
  displacing it. The category is idempotent — it detects the badge by its
  shields.io URL and skips when already present — and non-destructive — it
  never creates a README that does not exist. In the metadata summary returned
  by the default init call (no category), it reports its status so the calling
  agent can present "badge: would-add", "badge: already-present", or
  "badge: no-readme" without a follow-up call.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - "The badge is inserted at the correct vertical position: after the last
      consecutive line that starts with `[![` at the top of the file (the
      existing badge row), or at line 1 if no badge row exists. It never
      lands in the middle of a heading, paragraph, or frontmatter block."
    - "Idempotency is determined by checking whether the README already
      contains the shields.io badge URL substring. When present, the
      category reports `already-present` and leaves the file untouched —
      no duplicate badges, no content changes."
    - "When no README.md exists in the project root, the category reports
      `no-readme` and writes nothing. It never creates a README."
    - "The default init call (no category) includes this category in its
      metadata summary with one of three statuses: `would-add`,
      `already-present`, or `no-readme`. The calling agent can present
      this to the user without issuing a category-specific follow-up."
    - "When called with the category parameter, the tool writes the badge
      line directly into the README and returns a manifest entry with the
      file path and a `created` or `exists` status — same contract as
      every other init category. No file content is returned in the
      response payload."
  undesired:
    - "A badge injected inside YAML frontmatter (between `---` fences) or
      before it. The insertion logic must skip any leading frontmatter
      block before looking for the badge row or line 1 insertion point."
    - A README created from scratch just to hold the badge. The category
      exists to annotate an existing README, not to generate one. A
      project without a README has made a deliberate choice and the badge
      category must respect it.
    - A duplicate badge appended on re-run because the detection check
      compared the full Markdown link instead of the stable URL substring.
      URL parameters or link text may vary across versions; the shields.io
      domain-and-path fragment is the durable identifier.
    - "A badge inserted at an arbitrary position — after the first heading,
      at the end of the file, or between paragraphs — instead of at the
      top of the file in the badge row. Placement that ignores the
      existing badge strip looks like a rendering bug to the project
      maintainer."
    - A category that silently succeeds when the README is not writable or
      the working directory is ambiguous. Errors must surface as a status
      the agent can report, not as swallowed exceptions.
---

## Context

The init pipeline bootstraps every aspect of AIDE into a host project, but
the result is invisible to anyone visiting the project on GitHub or reading
the README locally. A developer scanning a repo's badge strip sees CI status,
coverage, license — signals that communicate tooling choices at a glance. AIDE
adoption is one of those choices, and the badge gives it the same visibility.

This module is a peer category inside the init orchestrator, sitting alongside
writeMethodology, installAgents, scaffoldCommands, and the other helpers that
each own one slice of the bootstrap. It follows the same contract: return an
InitStep with a planning status when the orchestrator calls without a category
parameter, and write to disk when called with the category parameter. The key
difference is that this category modifies an existing file (README.md) rather
than creating a new one, and it legitimately does nothing — returns a skip-
equivalent status — when the file does not exist, rather than treating absence
as an error.

The badge line is a Markdown image-link pointing at shields.io, styled to match
the teal-green AIDE brand color. The detection substring is the shields.io
domain-and-path fragment, which is stable across parameter changes, link-text
edits, and style variations. The badge itself is not canonical AIDE doctrine —
it is a branding artifact, so its content lives inlined in this helper's source
the same way the methodology pointer stub lives inlined in writeMethodology.

## Strategy

**Detection uses the URL substring, not the full Markdown link.** The stable
fragment `img.shields.io/badge/AIDE` survives changes to style parameters,
logo options, link text, and URL encoding. Comparing the full `[![AIDE](...)]`
pattern would break on any cosmetic edit and produce duplicates on re-run. The
substring check is a single `includes()` call against the file content — no
regex, no parsing. This directly guards against the undesired outcome of
duplicate badges from brittle detection.

**Insertion position is determined by a two-phase scan: skip frontmatter, then
find the badge row.** Phase one: if the file starts with a `---` line, advance
past the closing `---` and any blank line that follows. Phase two: starting
from the post-frontmatter position, count consecutive lines that begin with
`[![` — this is the existing badge strip. The AIDE badge is inserted
immediately after the last badge-strip line, or at the post-frontmatter
position if no strip exists. This places the badge at line 1 of the visible
content when there is no frontmatter and no existing badges, appends to an
existing badge row when one exists, and never lands inside frontmatter. The
two-phase approach satisfies the desired outcome for correct vertical position
and guards against the frontmatter-injection failure mode.

**Three reporting statuses map to three states the agent needs to distinguish.**
`would-add` (or `would-create` in manifest terms) means the README exists and
the badge is absent — the category will insert it on a category call.
`already-present` (mapped to `exists`) means the README contains the detection
substring — nothing to do. `no-readme` (mapped to `would-skip`) means no
README.md was found at the project root — the category respects the project's
choice not to have one. The three-way status lets the calling agent present a
meaningful one-liner in the wizard without needing to inspect the README
itself.

**The module never creates a README.** If the file does not exist, the helper
returns `would-skip` immediately. This is a deliberate asymmetry with most
other init categories, which create their target files when absent. A README
is a project-level authoring decision; fabricating one to carry a badge would
be presumptuous and would conflict with the undesired outcome that explicitly
prohibits it.

**The manifest entry follows the same InitStep shape as every other category.**
The step uses `name`, `status`, `category`, and `filePath`. When the status is
`would-create`, the `content` field carries the full modified README text so
that applySteps can write it during a category call. When `exists` or
`would-skip`, no content is returned. The category field must be registered in
the InitCategory union type and in the orchestrator's category enum — the
architect will need to wire this into both.

**The badge line is a constant inlined in the helper, not read through
initContent.** The badge is a one-line Markdown image-link, not a canonical
doc. Running it through the initContent chokepoint would add indirection for
no content-drift benefit — there is no second consumer of this string, and
there is no multi-line template that could diverge from a canonical source.
The line lives next to the insertion logic that uses it, same as the
methodology stub template lives next to writeMethodology.

## Good examples

A project with an existing badge strip in its README:

Before:
```
[![CI](https://github.com/foo/bar/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](...)
[![Coverage](https://codecov.io/gh/foo/bar/badge.svg)](...)

# My Project

Some description here.
```

After injectBadge runs:
```
[![CI](https://github.com/foo/bar/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](...)
[![Coverage](https://codecov.io/gh/foo/bar/badge.svg)](...)
[![AIDE](https://img.shields.io/badge/AIDE-intent--driven-0D9488?style=flat&logo=markdown&logoColor=white)](https://github.com/aidemd-mcp/server)

# My Project

Some description here.
```

The AIDE badge sits at the end of the existing badge row, before the blank line
that separates badges from the heading. The strip reads as a single visual
block.

A project with no existing badges:

Before:
```
# My Project

Some description here.
```

After:
```
[![AIDE](https://img.shields.io/badge/AIDE-intent--driven-0D9488?style=flat&logo=markdown&logoColor=white)](https://github.com/aidemd-mcp/server)

# My Project

Some description here.
```

The badge is the first line, followed by a blank line before the heading.

The default init call (no category) includes the badge in its metadata summary.
The calling agent sees a step like:

```
{ name: "README badge", status: "would-create", category: "badge", filePath: "/path/to/README.md" }
```

and presents it in the wizard as:

> **Badge** — this will add an AIDE badge to your README.md. Want me to add it?

On a project that already has the badge, the agent sees `status: "exists"` and
reports:

> Badge: already present in README.md — nothing to change.

On a project with no README, the agent sees `status: "would-skip"` and reports:

> Badge: no README.md found — skipping.

## Bad examples

A badge inserted before or inside YAML frontmatter:

```
[![AIDE](https://img.shields.io/badge/AIDE-intent--driven-0D9488?style=flat&logo=markdown&logoColor=white)](https://github.com/aidemd-mcp/server)
---
title: My Project
---

# My Project
```

The badge landed at line 1 without checking for frontmatter. YAML parsers will
choke, and the frontmatter block is broken.

A duplicate badge after re-running because detection compared the full Markdown
link text and the user had changed the link target:

```
[![AIDE](https://img.shields.io/badge/AIDE-intent--driven-0D9488?style=flat&logo=markdown&logoColor=white)](https://github.com/aidemd-mcp/server)
[![AIDE](https://img.shields.io/badge/AIDE-intent--driven-0D9488?style=flat&logo=markdown&logoColor=white)](https://github.com/aidemd-mcp/server)

# My Project
```

The detection should have matched on `img.shields.io/badge/AIDE` and returned
`exists`. Instead it compared the full link and missed the match because the
user had changed the trailing URL.

A badge inserted after the first heading instead of at the top:

```
# My Project

[![AIDE](https://img.shields.io/badge/AIDE-intent--driven-0D9488?style=flat&logo=markdown&logoColor=white)](https://github.com/aidemd-mcp/server)

Some description here.
```

The badge is visually orphaned — it looks like a broken inline image rather
than a status indicator in the badge strip. Badge strips live above the first
heading, not below it.

A README.md created from scratch just to hold the badge:

```
[![AIDE](https://img.shields.io/badge/AIDE-intent--driven-0D9488?style=flat&logo=markdown&logoColor=white)](https://github.com/aidemd-mcp/server)
```

The project deliberately had no README. The tool created a one-line file that
serves no purpose beyond the badge. The project maintainer now has a README
they did not author that shows up on their GitHub landing page.

A category that returns `would-create` with no `filePath` when the README is
missing, causing the agent to think there is work to do and attempt to write
to an empty path. The correct behavior is `would-skip` — the agent reports
the absence and moves on.

## References

- `src/tools/init/.aide` -- Parent spec defining the category contract: each helper returns an InitStep with planning status, the orchestrator collects them, and the agent walks through categories one at a time in a wizard pattern.
- `src/tools/init/writeMethodology/.aide` -- Sibling spec demonstrating the pattern of inlining a template string in the helper source (the pointer stub) rather than reading it through initContent, and the marker-based idempotency approach.
- `src/tools/init/installAgents/.aide` -- Sibling spec showing the created/exists reporting pattern and the principle that existing files are never overwritten.
- `src/tools/init/index.ts` -- Init orchestrator showing how category helpers are called, how their InitStep results are collected into the steps array, and how the metadata summary is composed.
- `src/types/index.ts` (lines 140-188) -- InitStep interface, InitStepStatus union, and InitCategory union defining the manifest entry shape every category must conform to.
